{"cross_mech":[],"family":{"alphafold_models":310,"candidate_reasons":["pfam_previous_unknown_name"],"characterization_status":"","context_evidence_count":null,"cross_mech":{"example_proteins":0,"protein_traits_record":false,"records_by_mech":{},"scanned":false},"curation_status":"SEEDED","demotion_reasons":[],"description":"Thoeris is a bacterial antiphage defense system, which consists of two genes, thsA and thsB, via NAD+ degradation [[cite:PUB00097806],[cite:PUB00101117],[cite:PUB00101118],[cite:PUB00160037]]. ThsA has robust NAD+ cleavage activity and a two-domain architecture containing an N-terminal NAD-binding domain (denoted as sirtuin-like or Macro) and C-terminal SLOG-like domain. In some instances, such as in B. amyloliquefaciens ThsA has an N-terminal transmembrane domain [[cite:PUB00097806]]. ThsB (also referred to as TIR1 and TIR2) is structurally similar to TIR domain proteins but without enzymatic activity. This domain is found at the C-terminal of ThsA proteins which adopts a Rossmann-like fold that binds NAD+/NAD+derivatives. It has been characterised as the SLOG-like domain which binds NAD+ derivatives such as ADPR molecules produced by ThsB, which drives an oligomeric state change in ThsA and activates the NADase activity (represented in [pfam:PF18185]) [[cite:PUB00097806],[cite:PUB00101118]]. This entry represents the NAD+-binding Macro domain from B. amyloliquefaciens [[cite:PUB00097806],[cite:PUB00101117]].","domain_architectures":9,"interpro_id":"IPR045535","known_evidence_count":null,"matches":534,"name":"Thoeris protein ThsA, Macro domain","partial_evidence_count":null,"pfam_id":"PF20016","proteins":534,"proteomes":453,"short_name":"ThsA_Macro","source_url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/api/entry/pfam/PF20016","structures":5,"taxa":1034,"unknown_status":"EX_DUF"},"inputs":{"cross_mech":"cross-mech-duf-examples-2026-10-10","members":"pfam-uniprot-uniref90-2026-10-07","protein_examples":"duf-protein-examples-direct-2026-10-13","worklist":"